## 6. RESPONSIVE DESIGN (MANDATORY)

The website must be fully responsive and designed mobile-first.

Breakpoints (suggested): 480px, 768px, 1024px, 1280px.

Layout behavior:

- Mobile (< 768px): single-column layout. Patient area uses a fixed bottom navigation bar. Doctor / Nurse / Admin areas use a top bar with a slide-in menu drawer.
- Tablet (768px – 1023px): two-column grids where useful; navigation adapts.
- Desktop (≥ 1024px): Patient area uses a top header navigation with a centered content container (max-width around 1200px). Doctor / Nurse / Admin areas use a persistent sidebar (placed on the right in RTL) with a main content area, and tables/grids use the extra width.

Requirements:

- No horizontal scrolling at any width from 320px up.
- Touch targets at least 44x44px.
- Tables convert to stacked cards on small screens.
- Modals become full-width bottom sheets on mobile and centered dialogs on desktop.
- Use CSS Grid / Flexbox and logical CSS properties (margin-inline-start, padding-inline-end, inset-inline-start, text-align: start) so RTL and future LTR both work.

## 7. ARABIC LANGUAGE AND RTL

The website must load in Arabic.

- Set <html lang="ar" dir="rtl"> in index.html.
- All primary screens must be RTL.
- Ensure navigation, icons (e.g., arrows and chevrons mirrored correctly), layouts, lists, inputs, and buttons display correctly in RTL.
- Use Arabic as the default language.
- English terms may appear alongside Arabic where medically useful (e.g., medicine names). Use dir="ltr" on LTR fragments such as phone numbers, emails, and English drug names.
- Use a locally bundled Arabic font (Cairo or Noto Sans Arabic via @fontsource).
- Build a lightweight i18n layer (a translations dictionary + useTranslation hook, no heavy library required) so English localization and an LTR switch can be added later without redesign.

